[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d4ef47-2283-5013-800d-55532771b590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411315} {:answer/id #uuid "69a086d9-ed8a-5d19-a711-31dea503c803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411324} {:answer/id #uuid "8488ff21-a2ea-5b43-889c-08cc212c8dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411338} {:answer/id #uuid "94a8c259-c943-5b5a-8249-ef29a64409c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411341} {:answer/id #uuid "a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411348} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e8b322-e012-50ae-b918-cd16e0ce7f37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2006d399-9a69-519f-8bb4-45f612aefe6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213141" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{0,1}}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,X⊃Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0,1}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0}\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,{0}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊂{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X\\,R\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊃Y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $⊃$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $⊅$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0,1}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0,1},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $⊅,$ we have ${0}⊅{0}.$ This is equivalent to ${0}⊄{0},$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d51ed98-ac24-5fc6-a3ec-b6f3ee2ab3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411306} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411331} {:answer/id #uuid "843aa144-29e1-5a7a-b5ed-0a15c64b58f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411337} {:answer/id #uuid "de98701f-4772-5961-b734-93e00e6687bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411364} {:answer/id #uuid "f09ba51b-bb92-5a76-b8d4-9b2465b49c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411331} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eed9603-46c9-5a60-91ed-069b81019517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fbac889-73d0-5b8f-a301-da4ef8cb327a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213127" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,3}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,3$\n- $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,3$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1=1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $3\\ne 3,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239771eb-1873-5866-a462-6727c30f35e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411301} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2c6f96-fc4e-5564-95d5-c89c5d1f7d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411312} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411361} {:answer/id #uuid "db9ed992-4a83-51d6-999e-2f07e0541fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411363} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a71d76-19a3-532b-ba22-51c4aa5690b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411361} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8e8452-662e-5d96-814f-930fa128fbd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77fae439-40b7-5f50-94ed-61df5583b377" :question/math-academy-id "q-213144" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∤y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2\\,R\\,4$\n- $4\\,R\\,6$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,6$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∤y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∤$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∣$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤$, we have $2∤4,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $4∤6,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $2∣6,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f77177ee-74b0-5681-92a8-ed6994ab9f9c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of Standard Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411289} {:answer/id #uuid "1c321d12-faed-5b6b-b6cb-b31615bfec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the ordered pairs are elements of the relation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411299} {:answer/id #uuid "83250d56-27d9-5d35-998f-7c225e26de93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411336} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ffa5ba-9fc3-5ce6-841a-f2088a84936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411359} {:answer/id #uuid "f5797c74-f7f7-51f3-9dfa-2fc0175bef87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411289} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee0f5da-3466-5854-93e2-01adf980becc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb1a702e-c18f-5fe7-b6f2-5f1506bc9396" :question/math-academy-id "q-213003" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{1,0}}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following ordered pairs is an element of the relation \"is **not** a subset of\" on $A?$ \n\n- $({0},{1,0})$\n- $({1,0},{0})$\n- $({1,0},{1,0})$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x⊈y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our ordered pairs in turn.\n\n- The ordered pair $({0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{0})$ is an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊈{0}.\\,✓$\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411325} {:answer/id #uuid "862baade-6c89-5f3f-a85b-756cfdc43c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411339} {:answer/id #uuid "8868f605-fc4c-52a6-a072-da3b71bc8621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411340} {:answer/id #uuid "d231e4f4-a503-5b71-aee4-7e628eb0ceaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411360} {:answer/id #uuid "df350fa9-2809-583c-a268-f8b8d3e8def1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5deadd58-de08-5459-bea5-ad5b2c6a1739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-213145" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,2}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,2$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,2$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣$, we have $1∣1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $1∣2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $2∤2,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cf3dd2-1d1e-571a-b1f1-4ac972be63d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411287} {:answer/id #uuid "256131e7-1b92-545f-8144-d6cc2b71dbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411302} {:answer/id #uuid "744c2bf9-2be2-5a86-9e71-395056b3825f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411326} {:answer/id #uuid "f21c9568-89f5-550c-aeca-3135fd1e4015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411372} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411381} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c640c9-599a-5e63-a2a4-02ba3cb94fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-212638" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={e,\\pi }\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x\\ne y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $e\\,R\\,e$\n- $e\\,R\\,\\pi $\n- $\\pi \\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411499} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd34f00-e35e-5bc2-9e2e-d848768a619a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411513} {:answer/id #uuid "c72a5b0e-4bd0-5a33-aae9-d61e9b22e3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411520} {:answer/id #uuid "d81e7ec7-8b44-5e18-b5c4-bed1de80a167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411524} {:answer/id #uuid "f3648a58-4da3-5f65-8f21-ee1b6cdfadce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411499} :answer-field/id #uuid "b34d899f-07f1-5450-a677-a95d2bf81727" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29a87319-221e-52be-a49a-7a7f42765bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130357" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam in a class of $25$ students\n- The number of cards drawn until a red card is obtained\n- The number of books published by a publishing house in a month" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $25$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although drawing a red card can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of books published cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c0fe61-4cd5-5060-819b-ff762f83aae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411492} {:answer/id #uuid "500babc9-c423-5d42-a392-144709fe6b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411496} {:answer/id #uuid "5481292b-246a-5d52-93ec-b37d3cc2c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411498} {:answer/id #uuid "e849fced-d809-50ad-a2d4-a3c41ddca395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411531} {:answer/id #uuid "f93fce79-3e49-557b-96a5-c10f504fd798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93fce79-3e49-557b-96a5-c10f504fd798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411540} :answer-field/id #uuid "d22af52a-6cb4-5c75-ae52-e745c13307b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46c22425-ee4e-5a56-9d8f-8f1ffa9ada86" :question/math-academy-id "q-84748" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ soccer players\n- The price of a pair of pants in a department store\n- The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ae41fa-236b-530c-90e6-fb5cf9abc0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411477} {:answer/id #uuid "4a98ac69-4946-50af-be1a-a87eda63d1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411493} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae6abc-b491-5c69-9fda-9c44145f964f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411497} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3a5183-df01-521a-be7e-92566b8b266a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411528} {:answer/id #uuid "de49179b-7a0c-5bcd-ab74-23a6fdd2bd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ae41fa-236b-530c-90e6-fb5cf9abc0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411477} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b85b9db-4b3a-5931-a044-422d92ddf7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8dd54609-ae0c-53a9-a2a1-7823f9aa6595" :question/math-academy-id "q-84747" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of times \"head\" is obtained when a coin is tossed $10$ times\n- The number of students in a class\n- The number of tickets sold for a concert in $3$ days" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of \"heads\" in $10$ tosses can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students in a class cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of tickets sold for a concert cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c395945-c14b-5e42-8504-6dd097d78101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411476} {:answer/id #uuid "291531dd-6f7e-59b2-9b00-3a0fc4cc8ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411482} {:answer/id #uuid "4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411495} {:answer/id #uuid "970d7ed7-c547-5138-9f77-c9dd3621443a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411512} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c5e69a-ff05-5da9-a145-db810e12de50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411495} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcab9192-f4e1-5668-9a13-95de83c01389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e06f09da-f1cd-5f64-925a-f4c6e513e373" :question/math-academy-id "q-130361" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of trees in a forest of $300$ trees that have bird's nests\n- The number of vaccines administered in a country during one season\n- The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of trees that have bird's nests among $300$ trees can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $300$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although receiving a vaccine can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2993011b-20a1-5970-adb0-721cadaaedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411600} {:answer/id #uuid "4ced8491-b081-5ef8-90f8-3698d8dd8753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411604} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa79523-2a8b-5ac4-a74a-e388c50d151f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411606} {:answer/id #uuid "7b481987-958d-5896-baf1-928789c0b1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411607} {:answer/id #uuid "e7535899-b380-5240-8de6-34d4d073db6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ced8491-b081-5ef8-90f8-3698d8dd8753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411604} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46cc03c-be0d-5f3c-966b-7d8ddd1839d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62944003-1237-5928-b405-2159dbe448f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-135308" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.03+0.72 \\\\ 0.07+0.18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.75 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff71f3f5-8383-5815-ba42-289f14e16b57" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f7679f-73f5-5a0e-a03f-dee0f63f3f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411622} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddca7f1-da8d-5b3b-a8af-f028a712c9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411629} {:answer/id #uuid "3e90ef8b-8d7b-59de-9f36-95572e6e5a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411633} {:answer/id #uuid "47f66e07-d1c0-5f79-82e3-fcb796015fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411636} {:answer/id #uuid "73e34a12-d804-5c4c-b0b0-abf70c77b752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f7679f-73f5-5a0e-a03f-dee0f63f3f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411622} :answer-field/id #uuid "2932e32f-ef14-5573-93fb-85f8c4797761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "579647d8-78b0-511b-8a94-882ec6fc1b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-212923" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $N$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x>y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $6\\,R\\,8$\n\nII. $7\\,R\\,2$\n\nIII. $9\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,1$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x>y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $>$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $≯$ (or, equivalently, $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\le $) are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $6>8,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $7>2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\le ,$ we have $9\\le 1,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff71f3f5-8383-5815-ba42-289f14e16b57" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f17869c-0ece-5ef7-a77d-d4d3abc9fe0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411626} {:answer/id #uuid "4de08473-7aaa-5afc-bd97-107dd067e857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411638} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb80eb9-1870-59ae-9a1d-0cda06ea7804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411659} {:answer/id #uuid "d24a8b3d-e9ae-577a-92d3-05c2bbc465f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411660} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc5035-39d1-5b29-9501-8255ecdfee6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc5035-39d1-5b29-9501-8255ecdfee6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411666} :answer-field/id #uuid "48ac91af-23e6-5125-89c4-136992dc2060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8c98d98-74bc-5116-9f06-75c38fdcf981" :question/math-academy-id "q-212926" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $6\\,R\\,3$\n\nII. $5\\,R\\,8$\n\nIII. $7\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,9$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $6∣3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $5∣8,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $7∤9,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d05194c-249d-54fb-91f1-f614c0f5e584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Non-Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d27a60-60f5-5754-a7bb-f26a821748ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411640} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8dfe07-93a1-57b4-9823-f9fcd6b4b485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411643} {:answer/id #uuid "665cfac7-9e55-5543-a679-b699d85f4d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411644} {:answer/id #uuid "9f73524d-1b4f-5845-812e-02bb16d1c56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411651} {:answer/id #uuid "eb20f44b-f74b-58f6-88d3-202204570fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f73524d-1b4f-5845-812e-02bb16d1c56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411651} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5bc513-fdb3-5851-90bf-ada4837ec06e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5b2a52b-fd77-5422-b1dc-3a9ca690060e" :question/math-academy-id "q-212983" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x≡y+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $0\\,R\\,2$\n\nII. $1\\,R\\,9$\n\nIII. $4\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,13$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ or equivalently, $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $6∣(x-y-4).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $6∣(0-2-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $6∣(1-9-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $6∤(4-13-4),$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd70172-7256-5614-bf05-3767b88a1cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411728} {:answer/id #uuid "a1851ac1-51c2-53a9-b604-327eb8a7e44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411762} {:answer/id #uuid "adec6066-5ccc-5ce4-bd6d-a02f3d4d86ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411768} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cc8f06-8966-54df-b620-04802f29078b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411781} {:answer/id #uuid "c5343ac3-3229-5247-ba18-b96c1194c0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5343ac3-3229-5247-ba18-b96c1194c0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411786} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab4e9c4-6a2c-5be8-9bcf-e0683aaf1263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "409399cd-9cd7-53bb-83f5-ffa99003572c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95407" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have a complex eigenvalue\n\nII. $A$ is not diagonalizable\n\nIII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26beaa3c-ed64-5711-ae43-5915ddfb5a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411700} {:answer/id #uuid "3400f53e-dd72-52d4-80f7-e75f042482c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411703} {:answer/id #uuid "56148229-ce9a-5da3-a832-6f2fba7b9721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411723} {:answer/id #uuid "7d45cde3-2e6d-51fc-a005-c567ebefb006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411744} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f5746-2e70-560e-b999-9a3896073130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56148229-ce9a-5da3-a832-6f2fba7b9721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411723} :answer-field/id #uuid "aafa25da-d701-5b8d-940d-a4b926749cbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d807e12-6960-57f6-b74d-aa3fe66f0f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-178271" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0$\n\nII. ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{3})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{3})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431e7942-0839-55b7-a404-74d95db870c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411981} {:answer/id #uuid "4687ae48-3f94-52e2-9dea-bc272c1082bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411982} {:answer/id #uuid "5d69edd9-6b87-5ce4-a237-f6f2f7e1d07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411985} {:answer/id #uuid "cde51bb4-f6f6-5104-86ea-3da2a88dc7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411994} {:answer/id #uuid "f8eb5ef4-1688-5731-842c-a125cdee48be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cde51bb4-f6f6-5104-86ea-3da2a88dc7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411994} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f36666-4b80-55b9-8c5c-1ce4a6ba661d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40dd9397-7325-5b4d-9df5-fd6f72af0a3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76978" :question/problem "Given that $7≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=7$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=7-19=-12.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-12)$ because $(-12)÷6=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c37123-a096-5bf6-b2a7-5752505e1833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411976} {:answer/id #uuid "26490cf8-9eff-5b3a-b0d3-8c64c57d3157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411978} {:answer/id #uuid "2c364ca7-bc65-5525-9cbb-b33295254414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411979} {:answer/id #uuid "5825c4b0-9c95-5b01-bd68-b59a82484df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411983} {:answer/id #uuid "c31f3f15-6b9f-50fc-afb7-ae7ee0f2806d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26490cf8-9eff-5b3a-b0d3-8c64c57d3157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411978} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfebcc4f-6a0a-526a-a3ca-b56faab77eef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e4ac9e6-575d-537d-b731-f3556414b8f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-103492" :question/problem "Given that $15≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=6$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=15-7=8.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣8$ because $8÷2=4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $4∣8$ because $8÷4=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $6∤8.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07424979-9774-53c4-849f-0c08fd54fc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411974} {:answer/id #uuid "08bd5615-539e-5361-90d4-ed7d60cee26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411975} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdc657a-27b5-5e42-929a-143a6d8eadb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411987} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba8fc9a-b908-579d-8f19-6c7445fb911f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411989} {:answer/id #uuid "eb35e354-c87a-5af4-b949-2eb9feda22c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08bd5615-539e-5361-90d4-ed7d60cee26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411975} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a66d7e4-64aa-595c-8772-a5f8626e54fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd2ee89b-0850-5297-a810-7e54facce40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-212522" :question/problem "Given that $-7≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=3$\n- $n=7$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-7-(-3)=-4.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-4)$ because $(-4)÷2=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $3∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176604ed-c837-5d39-bf7d-e8e2128262e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412055} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1ed2f9-1e46-534a-a381-abb6e053ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412056} {:answer/id #uuid "a2457563-b0a9-5e93-b34f-725229856d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412057} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd590b8-7f9d-5cdd-9e3e-23772a325ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412058} {:answer/id #uuid "e04a3a05-fb55-5020-b5ef-f2fc7abaa120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2457563-b0a9-5e93-b34f-725229856d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412057} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a0d3a7-d6ab-5c8b-9be2-dd9dc9a08b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03fcc7d2-8c99-53e1-b5b5-d7a0f5cd296f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42481" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$\n- $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-28$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=14-27=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-14+1=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14-14=0\\ne -28\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023017fb-407e-5bef-96fe-5ca4f9043072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412408} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7b32a8-3cec-559f-a17c-bf7b28c6fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412409} {:answer/id #uuid "578d705a-4c53-5798-b6ab-04cb9c9c2712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412412} {:answer/id #uuid "cb491f25-cc92-5958-9889-8d4238a0075e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412415} {:answer/id #uuid "de2cfeee-36d6-5198-8124-8b5f27baa495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7b32a8-3cec-559f-a17c-bf7b28c6fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412409} :answer-field/id #uuid "6af571c7-96ee-5d86-b8e5-c8021a9767c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79c14c2f-568d-5ff3-b628-6c8dd8d0639f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32169" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $5x-4y=0$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of  the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892c08a4-3464-5326-833d-0cac32b879f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412794} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfbf6a0-2987-5862-8e6d-5405fa26152f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412795} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9aacf5-3935-547f-8a01-96b4e4f8e273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412797} {:answer/id #uuid "d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412799} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a1cc24-066e-5fd9-aec3-63dcebd27b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412799} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7e02fc-389b-5a53-ab92-9e7d664ea877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71a6807f-51a3-5e94-a65a-0087aa5dc119" :question/math-academy-id "q-330333" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072d3215-6073-5918-a9fe-1dcc32903f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412793} {:answer/id #uuid "b96cdf9a-49a4-54e5-9dfc-848510bf8222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412796} {:answer/id #uuid "cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412798} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c4b1ce-27b0-544b-a5c2-9c96a3531721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412800} {:answer/id #uuid "eae566d9-a61e-5385-975a-ff789b6dc62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412798} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f03c56a-d1c9-5e79-89bf-0c51f2598a8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a0af42-71ea-5bb3-8eb8-b8b29ef8a2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-330354" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1a842b5-7256-59cd-b33f-d590bc315d84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a74b48c-8a09-5420-9014-3039dfa306ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412824} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3caf48-3d6b-58e9-8443-a76f248d4f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412848} {:answer/id #uuid "5f50b178-baf5-50bc-b791-e2b9c9ce601d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412849} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba5e226-5ed0-5aa6-8f25-569b9054ddde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412862} {:answer/id #uuid "c31a23b5-9755-5c42-94ff-b6938dbd6b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c31a23b5-9755-5c42-94ff-b6938dbd6b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412899} :answer-field/id #uuid "2372040f-636b-51db-a88a-0a20d411e932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f9012f2-83ba-5f6f-b553-2ea47d5d4f81" :question/math-academy-id "q-78640" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There are $3$ distinct residue classes modulo $6$\n\nII. $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}\\ne ∅$\n\nIII. $Z=[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}={[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $6$ distinct residue classes modulo $6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $5≢1\\,(\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2} & =[1+2\\cdot 1]_{2}=[1]_{2} \\\\ [6]_{2} & =[0+2\\cdot 3]_{2}=[0]_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2} & =[1]_{2}\\cup [0]_{2} \\\\  & =[0]_{2}\\cup [1]_{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d2f207a-8e99-5520-ab9f-266c8f54465b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to a Residue Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae50f18-3a44-5059-859d-62a308e0626c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412825} {:answer/id #uuid "536a2ad9-31ea-58bb-ad50-9a311d120bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412841} {:answer/id #uuid "6e813eca-88bc-51a0-a774-be6fb12da67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412854} {:answer/id #uuid "a3be6687-8c01-58a1-8b6f-55c990a13f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412883} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf198f1-51ce-5197-901f-4a57ec6ca534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3be6687-8c01-58a1-8b6f-55c990a13f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412883} :answer-field/id #uuid "86eeeda2-7dc4-5dc4-b9bd-b1e07b0c7e13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e36839-2675-57c4-abf9-91a0b0717003" :question/math-academy-id "q-214450" :question/problem "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[4]_{7}?$\n\nI. $-3$\n\nII. $6$\n\nIII. $18$" :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[4]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $4$ modulo $7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{7} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=4+7k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={4+7k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,4-2\\cdot 7,\\,4-7,\\,4,\\,4+7,\\,4+2\\cdot 7,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-10,-3,4,11,18,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $18$ belong to $[4]_{7},$ while $6$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1fe7ea-d91e-5440-b756-37a460a18f19" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Residue Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a9a1c5c-d379-566e-88ec-4f742d23a956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412830} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdd9f0e-932d-558a-9378-6f478cae4f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412831} {:answer/id #uuid "d3abf09a-f4e8-5b21-bd9a-3231221ada27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412910} {:answer/id #uuid "d5516742-1e80-5031-a6c5-e5f0e763ef73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412913} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8754b8-9881-5ed1-bb9f-dc739076a719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5516742-1e80-5031-a6c5-e5f0e763ef73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412913} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c42e18f-3211-5db3-867f-3fb18fe023c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "476816fb-a049-55c8-86b3-04683cf8a95e" :question/math-academy-id "q-78604" :question/problem "Which of the following residue classes modulo $6$ contain the integer $7?$\n\nI. $[-5]_{6}$\n\nII. $[3]_{6}$\n\nIII. $[1]_{6}$" :question/worked-solution "We have $7\\in [a]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\n7≡a\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $7\\in [-5]_{6}$ because $7≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $7\\notin [3]_{6}$ because $7≢3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $7\\in [1]_{6}$ because $7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1fe7ea-d91e-5440-b756-37a460a18f19" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Residue Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141ec379-3ffa-52e6-bad6-ca1691d2141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412818} {:answer/id #uuid "575da76a-a021-573e-9647-5cecb1252317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412844} {:answer/id #uuid "90050eab-d210-5e6f-ab25-dd473065930c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412875} {:answer/id #uuid "cf29b9c2-05c6-5263-a0ab-8a4e2db19757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412906} {:answer/id #uuid "def92c9e-e6e2-5c8e-91e7-4b7fb21509ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answ